    title: "Mutually unbiased bases in dimension six",
    statement: r`Determine the maximal number $M(6)$ of mutually unbiased bases in $\mathbb{C}^6$; in particular decide whether $M(6)=3$ or whether four or more exist.`,
    definition: r`**Mutually unbiased bases** (MUBs) are orthonormal bases $\{|e_i\rangle\}$, $\{|f_j\rangle\}$ of $\mathbb C^d$ with $|\langle e_i|f_j\rangle|^2=1/d$ for every pair of vectors. $M(d)$ denotes the maximal number of pairwise mutually unbiased bases in $\mathbb C^d$.

Complete sets of $d+1$ bases exist in prime-power dimensions. Dimension six is the smallest remaining case, and Zauner conjectured that $M(6)=3$. It is the flagship benchmark of N3. The sharp target was whether four or more MUBs exist, and ultimately the exact value of $M(6)$. Standard reference: Durt, Englert, Bengtsson and Życzkowski, Int. J. Quantum Inf. 8 (2010), [arXiv:1004.3348](https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.3348).`,
    progress: r`- Tensor-product constructions give $M(6)\geq3$, while the general dimension bound gives $M(6)\leq7$.
- Extensive numerical, computer-algebra and Gröbner-basis investigations have not produced a fourth basis (for example Brierley and Weigert, [arXiv:0808.1614](https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1614)). Numerical search failure is not a proof of global nonexistence.
- Restrictions on particular complex Hadamard families provide rigorous partial exclusions.
- In August 2026, Cárdenes Wuttig and Tindall claimed a complete exact finite-incidence classification of order-six complex Hadamard matrices, including Szöllősi's conjecture ([arXiv:2608.18053](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.18053)). This concerns individual matrices up to standard equivalence. A MUB set also requires simultaneous mutual unbiasedness between several matrices, so this classification does not determine $M(6)$.`,
    claim: r`**OpenAI, [“The maximum number of mutually unbiased bases in dimension six”](${OAI}preprints/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026/The-maximum-number-of-mutually-unbiased-bases-in-dimension-six-September-24-2026.pdf)** (OpenAI Math Release on GitHub, not on arXiv).

What it claims:
- $M(6)=3$, which resolves Zauner's dimension-six conjecture.
- The upper bound is computer-assisted: an exhaustive seed cover and a certified phase-ball atlas exclude four arbitrary bases. This holds under the stated binary64 arithmetic and compiler conditions for a complete run of the documented pipeline.
- A companion manuscript proves Fourier-vanishing certificates for order-six complex Hadamard matrices.

**Cárdenes Wuttig and Tindall, [“Using topology to reveal all mutually unbiased bases in dimension six”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06917v1)** (arXiv:2611.06917, v1), an independent second proof.

What it claims:
- $M(6)=3$ by a different route. The space of order-six complex Hadamard matrices other than Tao's is connected. This reduces the problem to finitely many overlapping phase balls, which interval arithmetic certifies to cover the space.
- A classification of every mutually unbiased triple: each lies in a two-parameter family or is the isolated Fourier triple, and none extends to a fourth basis.
- The cover and the quartet exclusion are checked by two independent interval-arithmetic programs. Lean proves the reconstruction, the connectedness and the step from ball certificates to the theorem.
- It reuses the graph-search tests introduced by OpenAI but reproves the underlying inequalities in Lean. The authors state that the proof relies on none of OpenAI's certificates.

Caveats:
- Two independent computer-assisted proofs now agree, but neither has been peer reviewed.
- OpenAI's Lean formalization proves only a five-basis bound, not $M(6)=3$.
- After an independent audit flagged that the exclusion code and run records were not public, OpenAI added a verification package on 26 October 2026: the C++ sources, saved numerical records described as matching a 4 October 2026 run, and a Python checker. Its own notes state that the checker is a consistency check of the saved records, not an authentication of the archive, that the build script does not reproduce the full computation, and that the correctness of the C++ implementation and of compiler and hardware behaviour are separate questions.
- No independent rerun or expert review of OpenAI's exclusion step has been found, and neither certificate set was rerun here. Hence “Claimed solved” rather than “Solved”, pending independent assessment, including of the floating-point certification.`,
