    statement: r`Can a dimension $d$ that is not a prime power admit $d+1$ mutually unbiased bases? As a separate quantitative programme, improve certified bounds on the maximal number $M(d)$ in specified dimensions.`,
    definition: r`**A complete set of mutually unbiased bases** (MUBs) in $\mathbb C^d$ is a family of $d+1$ orthonormal bases such that any two vectors from different bases satisfy $|\langle e_i|f_j\rangle|^2=1/d$. $M(d)$ denotes the maximal number of MUBs in $\mathbb C^d$, and $M(d)\leq d+1$.

Complete sets are known in prime-power dimensions. Whether any non-prime-power dimension supports a complete set is the sharp existence question here; finding every value $M(d)$ is a broader programme. N2 records the dimension-six benchmark. Current baseline: McNulty and Weigert's composite-dimension review, Quantum 10, 2051 (2026), [arXiv:2410.23997](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.23997).`,
    progress: r`- Writing $d=\prod_i p_i^{k_i}$, tensor products of complete sets give the lower bound $M(d)\geq1+\min_i p_i^{k_i}$.
- Links with finite geometry motivate conjectures (for example Saniga, Planat and Rosu, [arXiv:math-ph/0403057](https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0403057)). Projective planes of order 10 were proved not to exist by Lam, Thiel and Swiercz in 1989 ([doi:10.4153/CJM-1989-049-4](https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1989-049-4)).
- In August 2026, Wu, Liu and Wu reported standard tensor-product constructions in 64 non-prime-power dimensions up to 100 and extensive extension searches in such dimensions up to 30 ([doi:10.3390/info17080796](https://doi.org/10.3390/info17080796)). No extension was found. This tests chosen search procedures and starting families, not all possible MUBs, so it does not resolve any universal nonexistence claim.
- In September 2026, Cárdenes Wuttig and Tindall reported five MUBs in dimension 12, seven in dimensions 36 and 108, six in dimensions 48, 96 and 192, and ten in dimension 648 ([arXiv:2609.40311](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40311)). A Paley–Hadamard construction yields $q+1$ bases in $d=q(q+1)$ for prime powers $q\equiv3\pmod4$. These improve lower bounds beyond standard tensor products.`,
    residual: r`No complete set of $d+1$ MUBs is known in any non-prime-power dimension, and no fourth basis is known in dimension six. The constructive results of 2026 supply neither.

Complete complex MUBs are not known to be equivalent to finite affine or projective planes. Classical complete sets of mutually orthogonal Latin squares have their own established relation to planes. The nonexistence of projective planes of order 10 does not by itself exclude complete MUBs in dimension 10.`,
